OpenBSD specific client-server setup:
Cross-process memory access. In upstream Wine server can read/write another
process's memory with ptrace. On OpenBSD you can only ptrace your decendants.
Wine Processes are not descendants of WineServer so new APC_COPY_MEMORY memory
has the target process do the copy itself between its own memory and a shared
with the caller memory section.
The local NtReadVirtualMemory call handles both copy directions and faults:
view to address for writes, address to view for reads.
Added thread tid so server can name the target thread.
Leave SIGUSR2 deliverable during server calls so waiting threads can
still relay notifications; block it until the table and handlers are ready.
For new threads, libpthread owns the native stack separately from Wine's
signal-stack mapping. pthread_stackseg_np() returns the upper end; subtract
the size to record the base used for Unix calls.

Rebased: register every thread for the OpenBSD signal relay
(add_signal_tid() in init_first_thread and init_thread) and include
sig_openbsd.h.

Also: SYSCALL_NARROW_ARG() for the narrow (BOOLEAN/WORD) syscall
arguments in this file; see patch-include_wine_unixlib_h.

Also (openbsd-proton): APC_COPY_MEMORY copies with the uninterrupted
read/write helpers (page protections checked, no faults: the APC may run
in a signal handler, where a fault is fatal) and reports partial copies
like Windows.

APC_WRITE_MEMORY: small writes the server makes into this process (the
PEB BeingDebugged flag), because the server cannot ptrace() it.
init_process_done: set our own BeingDebugged flag when the server says the
process is being debugged.

server_call_unlocked(): claim relayed signals for the calling thread when
the call returns (patch-dlls_ntdll_unix_sig_openbsd_c).

